speechtext:
On the motion for second reading of this bill there has been an amendment moved, and I am somewhat concerned about the regularity of the amendment. No doubt it is based on citation 657 of Beau-chesne's third edition. The amendment proposes:
That the said bill be not now read the second time but that . . . consideration should be given to the introducing of legislation to amend the National Housing Act, 1944, to permit:

National Housing Act
(1)	Central Mortgage and Housing Corporation to issue loans directly to the builder at an interest rate of 2 per cent;
Does not that proposal go beyond the scope of the present bill? The rule of relativity must always prevail. The second part of the amendment reads;
(2)	Central Mortgage and Housing Corporation to subsidize public housing authorities in order that rents may be brought into line with the financial means of low-income dwellers;
It seems to me that there is a similar clause in the National Housing Act which the bill purports to amend.
The third part of the amendment reads:
(3)	Central Mortgage and Housing Corporation to enter the field of housing construction to build low-rental housing projects where such projects are needed but are not being constructed.
After reading the National Housing Act, it seems to me that the power is contained in the act at the present time. I would be glad to hear any representations which hon. members would like to make in respect of the amendment, but I thought I should mention those three features.
